425:
Even if the text "āpattiṃ jānāti" (he knows the offense) is not memorized, if he knows "by doing such a thing, one falls into this offense," it is permissible; so it is written. That meaning should be taken only after the text has been mastered before such knowledge; it is said that this is the same method for the teacher (ācariya) and the preceptor (upajjhāya).

## The Explanation of the Story of Kammārabhaṇḍu and others is finished.

## Explanation of the Story of Rāhula

"Venerable Sir, now the trees have blossoms as red as embers," with these sixty verses, (the Buddha) was asked to be shown. Here, the word "iti" should be inserted. "Pokkharavassa" is water the color of a lotus leaf; when that rain falls, only those who wish to get wet become wet. "Uṇhīsato paṭṭhāya" means starting from the top of the head.

He has soft, delicate, dark, and curly hair;
He has a forehead as pure as the surface of the stainless sun;
He has a nose that is well-proportioned, high, soft, and long;
He is the Lion of Men (Narasīha), radiant with a net of rays.

Or else:

He has beautiful red feet marked with the excellent wheel;
He has long heels adorned with the marks;
He has feet beautified by the fly-whisk and the golden parasol;
This Buddha is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

He is the most excellent of the Sakyan princes, delicate and tender;
He has a full body variegated with the marks;
He is the Hero of Men who has gone for the welfare of the world;
This Buddha is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

His facial complexion is like the full moon;
He is the Noble One among men, beloved by gods and men;
He has a gait as graceful as a majestic elephant in rut;
This Buddha is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

426:
He is born of the Khattiya lineage, more excellent than the highest nobles;
He has feet that are worshipped by the host of gods and men;
He has a mind established in virtue and concentration;
This person is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

He has a nose that is long, well-proportioned, and beautifully shaped;
He has eyelashes like a young calf's and very dark, beautiful eyes;
He has dark eyebrows curved like the bow of Indra (the rainbow);
This person is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

He has a neck that is more rounded and beautifully set than those with round necks;
He has a jaw like that of a lion king;
He has a pure body like that of the king of beasts, the Kesara lion;
He has an excellent complexion with skin like pure gold;
This person is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

He has a voice that is pure, deep, and melodious;
He has a beautiful tongue, as red as if smeared with vermilion and red arsenic;
He has twenty teeth above and twenty below, which are very white and pure;
This person is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

He has beautiful hair, as dark as the color of collyrium;
He has a forehead and temples as pure and beautiful as if covered with a gold plate;
He has a pure and bright ūrṇā (hair between the eyebrows) like the morning star;
This person is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

Like the moon traveling in the blue path of the sky, surrounded by a host of stars;
The Buddha, the Lord of Monks, walks in the midst of his disciples;
This person is indeed your father, the Lion of Men.

With these verses (he was praised). In the word "Uddiṭṭhe," the meaning is "one should practice in this way"; in his own (version), the Dhammapada text is "Uttiṭṭhe." "Dhammaṃ" refers to the duty of wandering for alms from house to house without skipping any. One should practice the Dhamma of good conduct, avoiding improper ways of seeking livelihood.
